WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.305 --> 00:00:08.430
CYRUS MISTRY: Hi, everyone.

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:09.097
AUDIENCE: Hello.

00:00:09.097 --> 00:00:10.520
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.560
CYRUS MISTRY: I'm Cyrus.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.340
I am the product
lead for Chromebooks

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:24.780
in the commercial segment,
which means education, business,

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:28.230
government, that whole
deal, everything other

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:32.380
than you walking into
Best Buy, basically.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:36.120
So here to talk a little
bit about Chrome Enterprise,

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.480
give you a sneak peek
into stuff that's coming.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:45.390
And then I will try to save
time at the end for questions,

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.270
because I think that's
actually the most fun part--

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:52.350
things you want to know about
how it works, interesting

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.700
behind the scenes stuff.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.590
There's mics up here.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:00.810
Or you can shout it out,
as soon as we get to Q&amp;A.

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.370
I'll try to leave
10 to 15 minutes

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:07.080
at least for Q&amp;A.
So just jot down,

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.370
Google Keep, whatever,
your questions.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:13.770
Oh, this is where I'm
supposed introduce myself.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:14.910
Oh, oops.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:18.390
That's a pretty interesting
black and white they did.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:23.240
So I've been at Google
13 years this year.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:29.860
That's a very long time
in Google standards.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:35.290
And I've been on Chrome
OS specifically--

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:36.860
next year will be 10 years.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:41.120
So I was on the initial team,
which is pretty exciting.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.770
So I've seen it,
seen the whole ride.

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:47.625
When I first joined they said,
what do you want to work on?

00:01:47.625 --> 00:01:51.370
Because we have two
product managers.

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:56.080
And I said my background
is in enterprise.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.470
I said, I think this
product will do well

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:01.390
in a managed
environment with IT,

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:06.880
because they love the idea
of a simple, secure endpoint.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:08.720
So that's what I did.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.770
I worked on the very
first prototype, CR48.

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:14.060
Does anyone remember the CR48?

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:15.380
No, yes, three hands--

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:19.600
[INAUDIBLE] three
people remember that.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:20.800
That's a long time ago.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:22.900
It's probably worth money
on eBay if you have one,

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:26.710
by the way, because
we only made 60,000.

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:30.310
I was given 16,000 to give
out to schools and businesses

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:34.750
to go try out and say, would
you like a device like this?

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:37.100
A lot of your employees
are living on the web.

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.590
What do you think?

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:42.683
And they said, yes, can
you please take my money?

00:02:42.683 --> 00:02:45.220
I like this much better
than what I'm doing.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:49.310
So we thought we
were onto something.

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:54.760
And it's been quite a journey
in the decade that's followed

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:57.400
and the stuff that we've added
and stuff that we've done,

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.960
so I'll talk a little
bit about that.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:04.540
I'd like to start with
a little bit of context.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:07.390
People wonder why are
you making computers?

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:09.480
You guys do all kinds
of strange things.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.460
Although now they ask
that less, since we have

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:16.515
driverless cars and all kinds
of strange things going on.

00:03:16.515 --> 00:03:17.890
The background,
just so you know,

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:19.700
this company was
born on the web.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:23.920
Everything we do is web, right?

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.840
And we want to make sure that
we give people the best web

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:27.340
experience.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:28.617
So we would optimize the web.

00:03:28.617 --> 00:03:29.950
We'd really push the boundaries.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:32.050
For those of you who
remember, the web

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:33.790
used to be, of course,
static web pages.

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:35.790
And then we slowly kind
of pushed the boundaries

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:37.480
to do actual
applications, right?

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.230
Gmail was kind of
one of its kind,

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:41.680
embedded chat,
Google Maps, right,

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.533
and the [INAUDIBLE]
actually can zoom.

00:03:43.533 --> 00:03:45.700
And all these kind of things
were very revolutionary

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:46.420
at the time.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:48.400
We were pushing the envelope.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.930
Bandwidth was still painful,
very painful for those of you

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:57.050
that remember dial tones and
what you had to go through.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:59.380
And so then we spun up
efforts to improve bandwidth.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:01.133
And that's come
a tremendous way.

00:04:01.133 --> 00:04:03.300
Two or three different
projects we've had, at least,

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:07.310
in bandwidth, and then it
became eight or nine projects.

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:08.930
So we had bandwidth.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:10.250
We had great web apps.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:14.810
And then you had this browser
that was just painful.

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:15.840
You had toolbars.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:17.450
You guys remember toolbars?

00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:19.730
People had three or
four different toolbars.

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:23.660
The Google toolbar
was very popular.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:28.100
And then big buttons
for back and forward.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.710
And that entire
part of your screen

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:32.540
took up 50% of your screen.

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:34.430
And then there's
this small portion

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:35.990
below that you would
actually scroll

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:38.810
to actually get to the content.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.150
And the part around
the actual content

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:43.410
was called the Chrome
of the browser.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:46.100
So of course, kind
of tongue in cheek,

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:47.420
we decided to call it Chrome.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:49.045
Because we actually
wanted to get away.

00:04:49.045 --> 00:04:51.470
Google's in a very unique
position, where we're not

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:54.290
actually interested in you
staying in our products

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:55.350
for long periods of time.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:56.480
Think of Search.

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:58.910
We win when you're
out of there fast.

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:01.220
You've got your answer
and you move on, right?

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:04.400
Same with Chrome-- our goal
is to get out of the way.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.600
Make the content
front and center.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.530
And so that's actually why
we went out and did Chrome.

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:11.750
We tried to work with the
other browser providers.

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:13.060
We worked hard.

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:14.220
It was very, very hard.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:19.108
And when we came out with
this in 2009 or 2008,

00:05:19.108 --> 00:05:21.150
a lot of people said, do
we need another browser?

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:22.670
There's already
so many browsers.

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:26.100
And of course now, Chrome,
I think most of you know,

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.290
is doing pretty well,
largest browser in the world.

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:33.200
2 billion people use the Chrome
browser on a weekly basis.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:36.520
It's just staggering when
you think about that.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:41.320
So we'd kind of
addressed web browsing.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:43.370
And then there was
the last problem.

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:47.440
The third big problem is you
would turn on your computer.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:50.260
And you would go to get coffee.

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:51.940
And you would go take a nap.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:55.660
And then you would come back,
and it would still be booting.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:59.260
And then when you finally got
in, startup would take forever.

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:03.100
You would log in and pop ups
would come in and annoy you.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:05.620
And you were just waiting to
click the Browser button to get

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:06.310
on with your life.

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:08.185
And a lot of people
can't even remember this.

00:06:08.185 --> 00:06:09.220
It has been a while.

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:11.780
This was the state of computing.

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:13.078
It would get slower over time.

00:06:13.078 --> 00:06:15.120
If any of you have a five
or six-year-old desktop

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:16.410
computer in your
basement, you know

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:17.702
exactly what I'm talking about.

00:06:17.702 --> 00:06:20.580
You probably never want to turn
on that thing again, right?

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:21.287
It's loud.

00:06:21.287 --> 00:06:23.370
You don't even know what's
going on in that thing.

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:26.950
This was the computing
world back then.

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:28.660
The cheapest
computer at the time

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:33.820
was $450 when we
started our journey.

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:37.470
And we came out with
this completely radical

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:41.100
$199 computing,
fastest way to get

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:44.852
on the web in the world,
five seconds to boot.

00:06:44.852 --> 00:06:46.560
So it was very
revolutionary at the time.

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.830
And a lot of analysts credit
the Chromebooks and the journey

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:52.290
we've been on for kind
of completely changing

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:53.440
how computing works.

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:57.980
And this is a little
bit of our journey.

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:00.480
It's a lot we've done
in 10 years, when you

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.420
look at Chrome and Chrome OS.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:06.668
When Chromebooks came
out, a lot of people

00:07:06.668 --> 00:07:07.710
don't even remember this.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:08.940
This is now way dating.

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:13.620
Chrome version 9,
I think, 8 or 9.

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:15.310
You didn't even have
multiple windows.

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:19.350
It was just literally a
single browser, full screen.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:20.910
That was it.

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:21.990
You couldn't minimize.

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:24.630
And as strange as
this sounds, it

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:27.666
drove people crazy that
there was no wallpaper.

00:07:27.666 --> 00:07:29.041
They said I want
to see my wall--

00:07:29.041 --> 00:07:30.791
and we said, well,
there's nothing to see.

00:07:30.791 --> 00:07:32.160
There's no applications to pin.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:32.650
There's nothing.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:33.420
It's just the web.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:35.890
And they said, well, I
still want to minimize it,

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:37.133
so I can see the wallpaper.

00:07:37.133 --> 00:07:38.550
There was this
very strange thing.

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:39.480
So we actually did that.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:40.980
We gave them the
ability to slide it

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:43.660
away and look at a picture.

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:46.620
But that was the very first
iteration of Chromebooks.

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:49.020
We then added all kinds
of features for schools.

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:50.520
We added kiosk mode.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:53.678
And we added this cool feature
called Managed Guest Sessions.

00:07:53.678 --> 00:07:55.470
It used to be called
Public Sessions, which

00:07:55.470 --> 00:08:01.500
was this completely ephemeral
way of running a Chromebook.

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:08.600
And we then added the entire
Play Store, which is crazy,

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:11.530
the 2 million or
whatever applications.

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:13.980
So you have the entire
mobile application ecosystem

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:19.450
on a full desktop browser,
which has also never been done.

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:22.560
We'd recently, which
we just launched in 73,

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:27.450
the enterprise controls for
full Linux application support.

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:31.450
So a lot has happened in
a short period of time.

00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:36.720
So let's go in and look
at some of the myths that

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:38.190
are out there about Chrome OS.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:39.150
I think these are fun.

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:41.820
First, you're only an edu play.

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:42.870
What the heck?

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:46.405
Well, first of all, I
can tell you about edu--

00:08:46.405 --> 00:08:48.930
phenomenal growth in education,
as you probably know.

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:52.380
We are number one
in many countries.

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:54.810
Every quarter to
two quarters, we

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:57.990
become number one
in another country.

00:08:57.990 --> 00:09:01.110
Almost 70% share in the
United States in education--

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:04.020
so phenomenal growth,
but the good news

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:07.440
is it's been growing in all the
other segments extremely well.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:12.690
This is the non-edu,
business growth of Chrome OS.

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:15.870
And you can see this
is a good trend.

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:17.880
We're on the right
side of the trend.

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:23.140
As companies move to the cloud,
this is exactly what they want.

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:26.880
They want a secure
endpoint that is fast,

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:28.930
that is shareable, et cetera.

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:33.150
So the rest of the PC market,
unfortunately, fortunately,

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:34.650
is fairly flat.

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:38.040
In many areas, it's
negative 1%, negative 6%.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:40.310
Overall in the
commercial segment,

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:44.200
it's right around
flat, 1% to 2%.

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:46.720
So this is very
good for us to see.

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:49.920
And the good news is it's
accelerating, not just staying

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:51.647
that way.

00:09:51.647 --> 00:09:52.980
A lot of companies are using it.

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:56.100
People ask, who's actually--
is this a real thing?

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:58.650
And they're using it for
a variety of reasons.

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:02.980
They're not just using it
for your front line workers.

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:04.980
There's people using it
for information workers.

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:06.692
People using it all
day long-- using it

00:10:06.692 --> 00:10:07.650
for content management.

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:08.930
They're using it for ERP.

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:12.720
They're using it for point of
sale, all kinds of use cases

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:15.160
out there.

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:17.400
And this is obviously
a very small sample

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:20.670
of some of the customers
that are out there.

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:25.380
Do you have enterprise
class Chromebooks?

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:26.540
Yes.

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:29.112
[INAUDIBLE] got a laser pointer?

00:10:29.112 --> 00:10:29.612
Oh, yeah.

00:10:29.612 --> 00:10:30.818
Of course, it won't work.

00:10:30.818 --> 00:10:31.860
Oh, it does kind of work.

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:36.300
This device is an example.

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:36.967
I was going to--

00:10:36.967 --> 00:10:38.592
I don't know if I
can mention the name.

00:10:38.592 --> 00:10:40.320
But I was going to
a large hotel chain,

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:43.460
and I went to their call center.

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:45.300
And they had thousands
and thousands

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:51.090
of people in this call
center, taking reservations,

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:52.860
handling support.

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:54.957
And I said, oh, this is awesome.

00:10:54.957 --> 00:10:56.290
What application are they using?

00:10:56.290 --> 00:10:57.540
What are they doing?

00:10:57.540 --> 00:10:59.490
And this was before
Chromebooks even launched.

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:00.810
I said what are they using?

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:03.562
And they said well,
they're just using the web.

00:11:03.562 --> 00:11:04.770
They just use Chrome all day.

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:06.030
And I said, oh,
that's fantastic.

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:07.030
They use Chrome all day.

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:08.370
What are they using?

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:10.870
They said, well, we've
given them Mac Minis.

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:13.560
I said Mac Mini-- that's
a pretty fancy thing.

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:15.120
And only in the browser?

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:16.530
Yeah, and he said, it's a pain.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:17.280
People take them.

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:18.060
They steal them.

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:19.480
[INAUDIBLE]

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:21.340
And so I said, my gosh.

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:22.410
That's [INAUDIBLE].

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:23.770
Do you want something better?

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:25.080
Because you just want the web.

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:29.520
And they said, yes, absolutely,
but we need a headless device,

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:31.860
because we have big monitors.

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:34.107
We want to have our
standard keyboards.

00:11:34.107 --> 00:11:35.940
So we don't want a
laptop form [INAUDIBLE]..

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:37.270
I actually went back--

00:11:37.270 --> 00:11:39.420
I went back to our team
and I said, can you

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:41.640
make me something small?

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:45.758
No battery, no
keyboard, no monitor--

00:11:45.758 --> 00:11:46.800
and we actually did that.

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:48.425
We actually came up
with the Chromebox.

00:11:48.425 --> 00:11:51.150
And the reason I gave that
story is because we actually

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:55.720
made these devices
for enterprise, right?

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:58.350
And there's a lot of them
now, almost all the OEMs

00:11:58.350 --> 00:11:59.040
make these--

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:00.610
Chromebox, as well.

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:02.430
But we've got Chromeboxes.

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:03.780
We've got Chromebooks.

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:06.000
We've got convertibles and 360s.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:07.440
We've got detachables.

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:08.700
We have tablets.

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:10.230
We have stylus.

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:13.470
We have all the way to I7 units.

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:16.950
We have core I5 view, where
you can get 16 gig units.

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:23.160
You can get Chromebooks that
are up to $1,500 Chromebooks.

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:27.870
Every major OEM
makes Chromebooks.

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:31.180
So you can absolutely get
enterprise class hardware,

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:36.480
any form factor you want, touch,
4k, all of those cool things.

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:40.010
So that's another
good thing to know.

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:40.510
Oh, good.

00:12:40.510 --> 00:12:41.120
Here we go.

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:41.620
Did I do it?

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:43.660
OK, good.

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:46.310
They don't really work offline.

00:12:46.310 --> 00:12:48.750
Gosh, the number of people
that have told me this.

00:12:48.750 --> 00:12:50.738
There is a lot of
people that actually--

00:12:50.738 --> 00:12:52.780
and I'm not kidding when
I say this-- probably 10

00:12:52.780 --> 00:12:53.740
times I've been asked this.

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:54.672
This is just amazing.

00:12:54.672 --> 00:12:55.380
How does it work?

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:56.650
So there's no disk at all.

00:12:56.650 --> 00:12:57.943
The disk is in the cloud?

00:12:57.943 --> 00:12:58.790
No, no, no.

00:12:58.790 --> 00:13:00.010
These are still computers.

00:13:00.010 --> 00:13:00.940
They have a disk.

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:02.260
They have everything offline.

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:05.900
You need a disk for caching and
all of those types of things.

00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:08.380
So it absolutely works offline.

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:12.100
The web has offline, of
course, assuming your web

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:14.680
provider has made offline work.

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:17.440
And then, of course, the entire
Android ecosystem is available.

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:19.450
Those applications, all
of those two million

00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:21.310
were actually built
offline first.

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:24.190
They all of them assume
offline, in a lot of cases.

00:13:24.190 --> 00:13:26.830
Almost all applications,
including web applications,

00:13:26.830 --> 00:13:28.780
will obviously work
better, in some cases,

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:32.290
work only on the web,
meaning when you're online.

00:13:32.290 --> 00:13:34.160
That's to be expected.

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:37.360
But getting developers to
start building offline first

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:38.695
is actually very good.

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:43.960
It's a good thing for
them to do anyway,

00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:46.480
because the applications
tend to be much quicker.

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:48.520
They work better in
flaky connections.

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:50.690
They handle things like
syncing, et cetera.

00:13:50.690 --> 00:13:54.040
So I usually encourage
developers, if possible,

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:57.750
build in that mode, in
that way of thinking.

00:13:57.750 --> 00:14:00.130
So there's tons and tons
of offline applications

00:14:00.130 --> 00:14:04.440
available on Chromebooks,
which is just good to know.

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:06.430
Does it only do the browser?

00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:07.140
Well, OK.

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:09.030
I think I probably
covered that a few times.

00:14:09.030 --> 00:14:11.070
Of course, it has the whole
mobile application ecosystem,

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:13.410
which again, is, I think,
a super cool unique thing.

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:15.990
When you think about where
people are building apps today,

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:21.030
no one is dusting off their
.NET and C# books and building

00:14:21.030 --> 00:14:22.340
for Windows.

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:25.410
They're building for the web
or they're building for mobile.

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:26.220
That's it.

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:29.063
And so the good news is,
when you think about that,

00:14:29.063 --> 00:14:30.480
it's actually
really, really great

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:32.022
to have the entire
mobile application

00:14:32.022 --> 00:14:35.310
ecosystem available, as well as
the full desktop browser, which

00:14:35.310 --> 00:14:38.280
is kind of very, very unique.

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:41.100
In addition, I mentioned
running Linux apps.

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:44.490
And it probably is worth
saying you can also

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:47.260
run legacy Windows applications
on a Chromebook, which

00:14:47.260 --> 00:14:47.760
is great.

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:49.930
There's lots and lots
of ways to do it.

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:52.620
There are providers that
stream Windows apps down.

00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:56.700
You can use on-premise
virtualization.

00:14:56.700 --> 00:14:59.790
There's even on-device
virtualization options

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:01.500
available now that
we have Linux.

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:04.050
And then you can run your
Windows applications,

00:15:04.050 --> 00:15:05.700
kind of a little bit inception.

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:08.882
But there's lots of
virtualization options.

00:15:08.882 --> 00:15:11.340
So if you really want to-- but
the main thing we want to do

00:15:11.340 --> 00:15:14.490
is for all of these, make sure
that it is ultra, ultra secure.

00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:17.070
None of these should open
up new security holes.

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:18.510
Everything is containerized.

00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:20.100
Everything is sandboxed.

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:22.770
Nothing gets access to
the OS in general anyway.

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:27.320
The OS is read only and blocked
from anyone updating it.

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:30.090
On a lot of other
operating systems--

00:15:30.090 --> 00:15:31.920
one of the reasons
viruses happen

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:34.740
and computers get slow
is because applications,

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:36.930
once they're installed,
become super users.

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:39.060
They can basically do anything.

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:40.400
They can modify the OS.

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:42.530
They can write to different
parts of the registry.

00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:45.395
They can just kind of
litter themselves all over.

00:15:45.395 --> 00:15:47.520
For those of you that
remember-- and it's very hard

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:48.930
to change this in an OS.

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:51.430
Although OSes are absolutely
all getting better.

00:15:51.430 --> 00:15:52.320
It's very, very hard.

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:54.900
Even uninstall was a big
problem in the olden days.

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:57.210
You had to go out and
find all of the bits that

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:59.460
might have been littered out
throughout your operating

00:15:59.460 --> 00:15:59.990
system.

00:15:59.990 --> 00:16:03.960
So this was just built an
extremely different way.

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:04.763
Hm.

00:16:04.763 --> 00:16:05.430
Oh, there we go.

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:07.620
Well, my clicker
sometimes doesn't work.

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:11.400
Maybe I need to stand here.

00:16:11.400 --> 00:16:14.830
What about regular updates?

00:16:14.830 --> 00:16:16.260
How does that all work?

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:23.030
So our update
mechanism is different.

00:16:23.030 --> 00:16:25.360
And I think this is one of
the extremely unique things

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:26.830
about Chromebooks.

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:29.830
Every Monday morning,
whether you're

00:16:29.830 --> 00:16:33.340
at school or at a business,
when all of your employees

00:16:33.340 --> 00:16:35.500
lift the lids and
start working, they're

00:16:35.500 --> 00:16:39.550
all running exactly the same
version of the OS, which

00:16:39.550 --> 00:16:42.160
is in itself remarkable.

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:45.020
All updates are delivered
entirely from the cloud.

00:16:45.020 --> 00:16:46.900
There's nothing to do
on prem, et cetera.

00:16:49.450 --> 00:16:51.790
But there's a few differences
with how we do updates.

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:54.760
First of all, all
updates are completely

00:16:54.760 --> 00:16:55.968
seamless to the user.

00:16:55.968 --> 00:16:57.260
They're done in the background.

00:16:57.260 --> 00:16:58.960
And the reason we can do
them in the background

00:16:58.960 --> 00:17:01.420
is every Chromebook has two
versions of the operating

00:17:01.420 --> 00:17:03.700
system at all times.

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:05.560
There's an A and a B copy.

00:17:05.560 --> 00:17:08.582
As you're running on
A, B gets updated.

00:17:08.582 --> 00:17:10.540
And of course, we don't
update the whole thing.

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:13.540
It's just delta updates
every six weeks or whatever.

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:17.170
Just the bits that need
to change are changed.

00:17:17.170 --> 00:17:20.530
As soon as we reboot, or as
soon as the user reboots--

00:17:20.530 --> 00:17:22.108
and by the way,
because it's so fast,

00:17:22.108 --> 00:17:24.400
this is another interesting
tidbit that a lot of people

00:17:24.400 --> 00:17:27.260
don't even know, most people
at Google don't know this--

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:31.720
because booting it's so fast,
because it's 5, 6 seconds, when

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:33.940
you log out and you get
back to the login screen,

00:17:33.940 --> 00:17:36.917
if an update is ready, we
will actually do a full reboot

00:17:36.917 --> 00:17:38.500
and take you back
to the login screen.

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:39.625
And most people don't know.

00:17:39.625 --> 00:17:41.910
They say, oh, that log out
took a few extra seconds.

00:17:41.910 --> 00:17:42.910
And they're back at
the login screen.

00:17:42.910 --> 00:17:44.868
But we've actually not
only done a full reboot,

00:17:44.868 --> 00:17:46.000
but we've swapped.

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:50.470
And we are now running
on OSB as the primary OS.

00:17:50.470 --> 00:17:52.770
At the same time
that we do that, two

00:17:52.770 --> 00:17:56.530
of the seconds of the boot are
actually taken up by something

00:17:56.530 --> 00:17:59.260
we call verified boot, which
is actually an entire process

00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:00.880
to verify the operating system.

00:18:00.880 --> 00:18:02.680
We verify every bit of
the operating system

00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:05.570
to make sure it is
exactly what we expect.

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:07.990
So in the event that we
have missed something,

00:18:07.990 --> 00:18:11.080
and something squeaked
through and was

00:18:11.080 --> 00:18:13.300
able to compromise
the OS, we won't even

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:14.620
let you boot with that OS.

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:17.655
We will revert
back to A and boot.

00:18:17.655 --> 00:18:19.030
So this is actually
all the stuff

00:18:19.030 --> 00:18:21.700
that goes on when you
boot a Chromebook.

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:25.940
And it all happens
completely in the background,

00:18:25.940 --> 00:18:27.340
which is pretty nice.

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:34.330
I think that's good on this.

00:18:34.330 --> 00:18:35.980
I kind of started
alluding to this.

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:39.520
How can they be so secure
if there is no antivirus?

00:18:39.520 --> 00:18:40.150
I don't get it.

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:42.850
And I cannot tell you how many
companies said I don't get it.

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:44.320
I really wanted antivirus.

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:46.390
And I said, well, I don't
know what to tell you.

00:18:46.390 --> 00:18:48.370
There's no such thing.

00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:51.250
You cannot have a virus.

00:18:51.250 --> 00:18:52.600
I shouldn't say that.

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:55.420
You don't need an
antivirus, as of now,

00:18:55.420 --> 00:18:58.900
because there is no such concept
as something compromising

00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:02.260
the OS in a persistent
way that wouldn't

00:19:02.260 --> 00:19:04.330
be detected when you boot.

00:19:04.330 --> 00:19:07.610
So one, how do we do this?

00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:10.300
And how has this even been
possible after 10 years

00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:13.718
of employees and
hackers and students?

00:19:13.718 --> 00:19:15.760
By the way, students are
some of the best hackers

00:19:15.760 --> 00:19:17.343
in the world because
they want nothing

00:19:17.343 --> 00:19:19.360
more than to use
their computer to go

00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:21.920
surf inappropriate things.

00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:23.620
And so it's really
fantastic that we

00:19:23.620 --> 00:19:26.290
have penetrated all of that.

00:19:26.290 --> 00:19:28.210
First is the OS itself.

00:19:28.210 --> 00:19:30.400
It's kind of kept
completely locked down.

00:19:30.400 --> 00:19:33.100
Nothing gets access to the OS.

00:19:33.100 --> 00:19:35.620
Again, I can tell you all
kinds of interesting stories,

00:19:35.620 --> 00:19:37.287
since I've been on
the team for so long.

00:19:37.287 --> 00:19:40.332
But there's a small shell
that you can actually run,

00:19:40.332 --> 00:19:41.290
called the cross-shell.

00:19:41.290 --> 00:19:42.873
For those of you
that know about this,

00:19:42.873 --> 00:19:44.770
we can actually run
some basic commands.

00:19:44.770 --> 00:19:47.443
They didn't take an
entire operating system

00:19:47.443 --> 00:19:49.110
and then build Chrome
OS on top of that.

00:19:49.110 --> 00:19:50.090
They actually did the opposite.

00:19:50.090 --> 00:19:51.632
They said we need
the Chrome browser.

00:19:51.632 --> 00:19:53.980
What are the minimum pieces
we need underneath it

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:55.660
to make it work, period.

00:19:55.660 --> 00:19:57.480
That's what we're
going to put in.

00:19:57.480 --> 00:19:59.230
The command ping,
for those of you

00:19:59.230 --> 00:20:02.080
that know what that command is,
the most basic, simple command

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:03.790
in the world, there
was an email thread

00:20:03.790 --> 00:20:06.460
about whether we
should allow ping.

00:20:06.460 --> 00:20:09.160
They look with that
level of detail,

00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:10.840
because every
single thing you add

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:13.510
is potentially another
vector, another threat.

00:20:13.510 --> 00:20:17.800
So they're very, very, very
careful about what they add.

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:19.570
Nothing can get
into the operating

00:20:19.570 --> 00:20:21.640
system of any channels
open to get access

00:20:21.640 --> 00:20:22.730
to the operating system.

00:20:22.730 --> 00:20:28.890
It's only done using private
Google APIs that we have.

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:31.050
The second thing we do
is we sandbox everything.

00:20:31.050 --> 00:20:32.880
We sandbox the entire
browser process.

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:35.520
Within the browser process,
we sandbox each tab.

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:37.710
The entire Android
container is sandboxed.

00:20:37.710 --> 00:20:39.362
Every app within the
Android container,

00:20:39.362 --> 00:20:40.570
then, in a separate sandbox--

00:20:40.570 --> 00:20:42.630
the thing is
defense in depth is,

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:44.910
of course, used in security,
in physical security

00:20:44.910 --> 00:20:46.260
and in software security.

00:20:46.260 --> 00:20:48.450
This is exactly what you want.

00:20:48.450 --> 00:20:51.300
Things would have to hop
out of many, many containers

00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:53.607
and sandboxes to get access.

00:20:53.607 --> 00:20:55.440
Then I mentioned, we
also have verified boot

00:20:55.440 --> 00:20:58.380
in case we've missed something.

00:20:58.380 --> 00:21:00.540
And of course, we
have humans that

00:21:00.540 --> 00:21:02.833
do all kinds of reviewing
of extensions and apps.

00:21:02.833 --> 00:21:04.500
We also have-- which
is pretty cool-- we

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:08.400
have a lot of AI and ML
that actually reviews all

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:10.500
of the application and
application updates

00:21:10.500 --> 00:21:12.960
that come into Play
Store, in the web store.

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:15.630
Things are automatically taken
down before a human even sees

00:21:15.630 --> 00:21:17.702
it, which is also pretty cool--

00:21:17.702 --> 00:21:19.410
which is very hard to
do given the scale.

00:21:22.622 --> 00:21:24.080
Is this an enterprise
class device?

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:25.820
Can I run this in my company?

00:21:25.820 --> 00:21:29.240
Well, the good news is, yes.

00:21:29.240 --> 00:21:30.950
There's a lot of stuff in there.

00:21:30.950 --> 00:21:35.540
There is 200 plus different
policies that you can set.

00:21:35.540 --> 00:21:39.140
The good news is almost no one
sets anything close to that.

00:21:39.140 --> 00:21:42.560
They need, like, 15 or 16
different controls, at most.

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:45.800
But the options are there if you
need a lot of advanced stuff.

00:21:45.800 --> 00:21:47.900
There's full fleet
management here.

00:21:47.900 --> 00:21:49.970
But the nice thing I
will say about this

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:56.220
is the stuff that's in here
is entirely cloud-based.

00:21:56.220 --> 00:22:01.730
So the entire management of your
fleet is entirely cloud-based.

00:22:01.730 --> 00:22:08.170
A lot of people don't know
this, but our management system,

00:22:08.170 --> 00:22:11.680
it's the largest cloud-based EMM
in the world, or MDM, for those

00:22:11.680 --> 00:22:13.030
of you who know what that is.

00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:16.780
No cloud EMM in the world
manages as many devices

00:22:16.780 --> 00:22:18.940
as this one does.

00:22:18.940 --> 00:22:22.390
Tens of millions of
active devices running,

00:22:22.390 --> 00:22:25.600
another 10 million
devices just in education

00:22:25.600 --> 00:22:28.750
gets enrolled into the
console in just a period

00:22:28.750 --> 00:22:31.032
of a few months,
July and August.

00:22:31.032 --> 00:22:32.740
And Google doesn't
feel it at all, right?

00:22:32.740 --> 00:22:34.615
It's one of the benefits
of having the scale.

00:22:34.615 --> 00:22:38.170
The IT administrator can be on
their phone on their car ride

00:22:38.170 --> 00:22:41.560
home, hopefully not while
driving, and can actually

00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:45.430
go in and make changes, look at
their fleet, disable a device,

00:22:45.430 --> 00:22:46.833
lock one down if it's stolen--

00:22:46.833 --> 00:22:47.750
those types of things.

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:50.415
You can do all of that entirely
from here, which is nice.

00:22:50.415 --> 00:22:52.540
Provisioning a Chromebook
and rolling a Chromebook,

00:22:52.540 --> 00:22:54.123
for those of you
that have done it is,

00:22:54.123 --> 00:22:57.610
I think, about seven seconds.

00:22:57.610 --> 00:23:00.420
Think about a different
OS, I won't say who,

00:23:00.420 --> 00:23:02.640
how long that takes
to provision that

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:05.130
and enroll that and
image that and get that

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:07.680
with all the applications
that you need.

00:23:07.680 --> 00:23:10.060
It's not seven seconds.

00:23:10.060 --> 00:23:12.150
So that's pretty exciting.

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:13.650
And of course, all
the applications,

00:23:13.650 --> 00:23:15.990
extensions, everything
you do is done from here,

00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:17.890
as well, which is kind of nice.

00:23:17.890 --> 00:23:19.530
And I think this may
be the last myth.

00:23:19.530 --> 00:23:20.320
I don't know.

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:22.680
Let's see.

00:23:22.680 --> 00:23:26.940
Can you integrate with
your legacy IT world?

00:23:26.940 --> 00:23:30.197
Because I have a legacy,
and how do I handle that?

00:23:30.197 --> 00:23:32.280
Every company does though,
which is the good news.

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:35.520
Unless you're a small
business, very small SMB,

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:40.060
or you grew up in a serverless
company, which the new ones do,

00:23:40.060 --> 00:23:44.070
fortunately, most new companies
don't bring out big servers

00:23:44.070 --> 00:23:47.490
and stick them
under their desks.

00:23:47.490 --> 00:23:49.240
So yes, there's lots
of options here.

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:51.300
First of all, you can
actually, if you really

00:23:51.300 --> 00:23:56.370
love Active Directory and
SCCM and group policy,

00:23:56.370 --> 00:23:59.220
you can manage your
Chromebook using that.

00:23:59.220 --> 00:24:03.000
It's more painful, name
value pairs, and JSON blobs.

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:05.670
But if you really
love that, that

00:24:05.670 --> 00:24:07.934
can be how you manage
your Chromebooks.

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:14.130
You can, of course,
access MS Office

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:15.670
if you need for your employees.

00:24:15.670 --> 00:24:16.740
There's actually lots
of different ways

00:24:16.740 --> 00:24:17.470
to do that, of course.

00:24:17.470 --> 00:24:19.020
There's native web
ways to do that.

00:24:19.020 --> 00:24:20.700
There's Android
versions to do that.

00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:22.490
There's the full
Office functionality

00:24:22.490 --> 00:24:23.440
if you really need--

00:24:23.440 --> 00:24:27.270
that team that needs the Excel
macros and their VB stuff

00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:29.590
from 1997.

00:24:29.590 --> 00:24:30.750
So all of that is there.

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:38.060
And SSO certificates,
802.1a ethernet--

00:24:38.060 --> 00:24:40.920
all of the stuff that
you think you might need.

00:24:40.920 --> 00:24:41.570
Are we done?

00:24:41.570 --> 00:24:42.070
No.

00:24:42.070 --> 00:24:45.630
There's an enormous list of
stuff that companies need.

00:24:45.630 --> 00:24:48.090
But the good news is,
whenever we talk to--

00:24:48.090 --> 00:24:50.910
almost every CIO
I've ever talked to,

00:24:50.910 --> 00:24:53.460
I don't need to
convince them that this

00:24:53.460 --> 00:24:54.657
is a better model for them.

00:24:54.657 --> 00:24:57.240
Anyone that's ever tried it and
tested it is, like, I love it.

00:24:57.240 --> 00:24:58.560
I want to expand this.

00:24:58.560 --> 00:25:00.840
But they say, this
is the stuff I need.

00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:01.500
Right?

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:04.020
So that's exactly the
right place to be.

00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:05.850
We are in exactly
the right situation

00:25:05.850 --> 00:25:07.980
because we're on the
right side of the trend.

00:25:07.980 --> 00:25:10.828
We already have
natural pull from CIOs.

00:25:10.828 --> 00:25:12.870
So it's just a matter of
making sure this has all

00:25:12.870 --> 00:25:14.020
the enterprise capabilities.

00:25:14.020 --> 00:25:15.540
There's an entire
dedicated team that

00:25:15.540 --> 00:25:17.970
just works on making sure
you've got all of the enterprise

00:25:17.970 --> 00:25:19.560
functionality you need.

00:25:19.560 --> 00:25:21.200
Of course, you
could run it today.

00:25:21.200 --> 00:25:22.680
You'd have to do some
work around the center,

00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:24.960
but we want you to be able
to run it with everything

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:27.630
that you possibly need.

00:25:27.630 --> 00:25:28.130
OK.

00:25:28.130 --> 00:25:29.420
Let me see what is next.

00:25:29.420 --> 00:25:30.020
OK.

00:25:30.020 --> 00:25:30.870
What's coming up?

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:36.390
Now the sneak peeks,
now no photos.

00:25:36.390 --> 00:25:37.220
Is what true?

00:25:37.220 --> 00:25:37.923
No photos?

00:25:37.923 --> 00:25:38.840
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:38.840 --> 00:25:39.620
CYRUS MISTRY: No.

00:25:39.620 --> 00:25:41.030
No photos for this part--

00:25:41.030 --> 00:25:45.450
there will be some very
exciting stuff here.

00:25:45.450 --> 00:25:50.510
So this part here is a little
bit, a sneak into what's coming

00:25:50.510 --> 00:25:55.050
and how we think about
the vision of Chromebooks

00:25:55.050 --> 00:25:56.070
in the future.

00:25:56.070 --> 00:25:58.560
When we think about everything
that we're building,

00:25:58.560 --> 00:26:00.660
we really think
about two sections.

00:26:00.660 --> 00:26:03.310
The first is, of course, we're
talking about for the end user.

00:26:03.310 --> 00:26:04.620
That's where our life begins.

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:05.940
That's our world.

00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:08.490
Secure and productive,
those are the two things

00:26:08.490 --> 00:26:10.170
that we want to make
sure that they are.

00:26:10.170 --> 00:26:12.170
They're secure and
they're productive.

00:26:12.170 --> 00:26:12.670
OK?

00:26:15.225 --> 00:26:16.850
And we'll talk about
those in a second.

00:26:16.850 --> 00:26:20.000
And the second piece, of
course, is the IT department.

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:23.180
Many times, unfortunately, more
important than the end user.

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:25.820
Because they're also the
ones buying and deciding

00:26:25.820 --> 00:26:28.230
and deploying and
doing all of that,

00:26:28.230 --> 00:26:31.350
making sure that they have
very powerful, easy to use,

00:26:31.350 --> 00:26:33.090
and management is
very important.

00:26:33.090 --> 00:26:34.580
And we'll talk about both
of these really quick.

00:26:34.580 --> 00:26:36.330
And I'll give you some
glimpses into stuff

00:26:36.330 --> 00:26:39.930
that is coming, which
I think is pretty cool.

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:43.310
Let's talk briefly
about the end user.

00:26:43.310 --> 00:26:47.210
The first thing
is very assistive.

00:26:50.815 --> 00:26:52.190
And when I say
assistive, I don't

00:26:52.190 --> 00:26:54.677
mean the Google Assistant,
which is amazingly cool.

00:26:54.677 --> 00:26:55.760
You can ask it a question.

00:26:55.760 --> 00:26:57.950
You can do hands-free
assistant now on Chromebooks.

00:26:57.950 --> 00:27:00.725
And you can just say the
magic words, which I won't say

00:27:00.725 --> 00:27:02.620
or everyone here with
an Android phone--

00:27:02.620 --> 00:27:05.540
it'll listen to my voice.

00:27:05.540 --> 00:27:07.303
But being able to
ask good questions

00:27:07.303 --> 00:27:08.220
and all that is great.

00:27:08.220 --> 00:27:10.460
But what we mean here is, when
you think about an operating

00:27:10.460 --> 00:27:11.960
system today, every
operating system

00:27:11.960 --> 00:27:13.910
we've ever used, whether
you're using Windows

00:27:13.910 --> 00:27:16.070
or you're using a
fruit or whatever

00:27:16.070 --> 00:27:20.630
you're using, when you use it,
it's a very reactive system.

00:27:20.630 --> 00:27:21.668
It's just waiting.

00:27:21.668 --> 00:27:22.460
It's sitting there.

00:27:22.460 --> 00:27:26.600
And it says tell me, wait
for me to click something.

00:27:26.600 --> 00:27:28.160
And it's not helpful.

00:27:28.160 --> 00:27:32.090
It should actually know you're
about to enter the meeting

00:27:32.090 --> 00:27:35.210
that you entered the same time
yesterday with the same three

00:27:35.210 --> 00:27:36.500
people.

00:27:36.500 --> 00:27:38.900
And it should just nudge you
and say, hey, by the way,

00:27:38.900 --> 00:27:40.850
this is the presentation you
guys were working on last time.

00:27:40.850 --> 00:27:42.140
Do you want to open it?

00:27:42.140 --> 00:27:44.660
Right?

00:27:44.660 --> 00:27:48.140
It should do it only if it has
an extremely high confidence

00:27:48.140 --> 00:27:49.430
level that that's what it is.

00:27:49.430 --> 00:27:52.550
That's how all AI and ML is
all just based on probability.

00:27:52.550 --> 00:27:54.930
But that's actually
what it should do.

00:27:54.930 --> 00:27:57.510
These are simple
things to do, actually,

00:27:57.510 --> 00:28:02.145
if you are a company with
AI and ML instinctively.

00:28:02.145 --> 00:28:04.020
But these are the types
of things that you're

00:28:04.020 --> 00:28:04.710
going to start seeing.

00:28:04.710 --> 00:28:06.600
You're going to see an
operating system that

00:28:06.600 --> 00:28:08.430
is actually going to help you.

00:28:08.430 --> 00:28:12.018
Now interestingly, by the way,
there's a lot of AI and ML

00:28:12.018 --> 00:28:14.310
actually within a Chromebook
today that a lot of people

00:28:14.310 --> 00:28:14.640
don't know.

00:28:14.640 --> 00:28:16.380
There's amazingly
cool stuff in there.

00:28:16.380 --> 00:28:20.200
Not just machine learning
around your preferences

00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:23.190
around brightness and time of
day and how much you like it,

00:28:23.190 --> 00:28:26.610
adjusting it, your
personal habits

00:28:26.610 --> 00:28:28.920
when you're running different
apps for what we should

00:28:28.920 --> 00:28:32.520
discard, how to optimize your
battery life, your performance,

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:34.050
when we should crank it up.

00:28:34.050 --> 00:28:36.540
All of those types of
things are actually--

00:28:36.540 --> 00:28:40.500
is actually on-device ML that
is running those things, to make

00:28:40.500 --> 00:28:42.100
it smart in the background.

00:28:42.100 --> 00:28:44.790
But we want smart
even for the end user

00:28:44.790 --> 00:28:48.480
directly for their applications.

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:51.870
The next one is productive.

00:28:51.870 --> 00:28:55.190
It's also extremely important
for those of you that

00:28:55.190 --> 00:29:00.260
live like me with 29 tabs
open, or probably 70,

00:29:00.260 --> 00:29:02.510
and they all just become
these tiny little icons.

00:29:02.510 --> 00:29:06.260
Does anyone use a lot
of tabs like I do?

00:29:06.260 --> 00:29:06.830
Yes.

00:29:06.830 --> 00:29:07.930
It drives me insane.

00:29:07.930 --> 00:29:09.680
First of all, I can't
even see what it is.

00:29:09.680 --> 00:29:11.480
And that also drives me insane.

00:29:11.480 --> 00:29:14.120
So fixing that,
that is all coming.

00:29:14.120 --> 00:29:14.680
Do not worry.

00:29:14.680 --> 00:29:17.180
There's absolutely going to be
better tab management and tab

00:29:17.180 --> 00:29:18.810
groupings and all
kinds of things.

00:29:18.810 --> 00:29:20.510
But even taking
it a step further

00:29:20.510 --> 00:29:22.630
to be able to have, of
course, multiple desktops,

00:29:22.630 --> 00:29:23.690
this has been
around an operating

00:29:23.690 --> 00:29:24.710
system for a long time.

00:29:24.710 --> 00:29:26.127
But doing even
cooler things, like

00:29:26.127 --> 00:29:28.610
if you are in a different mode
or in a different workspace,

00:29:28.610 --> 00:29:30.068
being able to take
all of that work

00:29:30.068 --> 00:29:31.310
and name it as a workspace.

00:29:31.310 --> 00:29:34.070
Keep it synced on any
device that you use.

00:29:34.070 --> 00:29:35.270
Get into focus mode.

00:29:35.270 --> 00:29:37.080
So right now I'm only
in this workspace

00:29:37.080 --> 00:29:39.080
and I don't want to be
disturbed or accidentally

00:29:39.080 --> 00:29:40.190
go to any other things.

00:29:40.190 --> 00:29:44.270
All of those types of things,
we have seen that they work.

00:29:44.270 --> 00:29:47.660
And so you should see a lot
of this cool stuff coming out.

00:29:47.660 --> 00:29:49.230
Flexibility, of
course, is important.

00:29:49.230 --> 00:29:50.240
I've already covered this slide.

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:51.950
But just want to
bring it up again,

00:29:51.950 --> 00:29:54.470
that it's very important that
people work the way they like.

00:29:54.470 --> 00:29:56.762
If you are the type of person
that really prefers touch

00:29:56.762 --> 00:29:58.445
or if you really
prefer a tablet,

00:29:58.445 --> 00:30:00.163
or you really prefer
detachable and you

00:30:00.163 --> 00:30:02.330
want to take out the keyboard
every once in a while,

00:30:02.330 --> 00:30:05.180
you should be able
to have that option.

00:30:05.180 --> 00:30:08.310
And then IT, what's
coming in this space--

00:30:08.310 --> 00:30:13.200
so two things that
I care about--

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:15.670
I want to do everything
I can possibly do.

00:30:15.670 --> 00:30:17.130
And I want it to be easy to use.

00:30:17.130 --> 00:30:19.620
When you think about what
IT departments care about,

00:30:19.620 --> 00:30:21.920
they usually want two things.

00:30:21.920 --> 00:30:24.330
They want visibility,
and they want control.

00:30:24.330 --> 00:30:26.685
Visibility means I just want
to be able to see and know

00:30:26.685 --> 00:30:27.810
everything that's going on.

00:30:27.810 --> 00:30:28.935
What are people installing?

00:30:28.935 --> 00:30:29.770
What are they doing?

00:30:29.770 --> 00:30:31.020
Why are they driving me crazy?

00:30:31.020 --> 00:30:34.020
What security things
are they doing now?

00:30:34.020 --> 00:30:38.170
And they want to make
it really, really,

00:30:38.170 --> 00:30:41.500
really easy to
control those things.

00:30:41.500 --> 00:30:44.550
So first thing is simple.

00:30:44.550 --> 00:30:45.680
I mentioned this before.

00:30:45.680 --> 00:30:47.700
We have, obviously,
a ton of policies.

00:30:47.700 --> 00:30:49.250
But we also do
something very simple

00:30:49.250 --> 00:30:51.650
called smart defaults,
where we actually

00:30:51.650 --> 00:30:54.920
go through every single policy
and make sure that it's already

00:30:54.920 --> 00:30:57.200
set to what we think would
be the best experience

00:30:57.200 --> 00:30:58.670
and the most secure for you.

00:30:58.670 --> 00:31:02.390
We always err on the side
of the more secure option,

00:31:02.390 --> 00:31:06.620
and then let you turn
things on as you want.

00:31:06.620 --> 00:31:09.410
This means you don't need
to go mess with 50 things

00:31:09.410 --> 00:31:10.160
to get it running.

00:31:10.160 --> 00:31:12.630
You can actually
do none of them.

00:31:12.630 --> 00:31:14.690
And then you can slowly
decide which things

00:31:14.690 --> 00:31:17.870
you want to turn on,
control, turn off, et cetera.

00:31:17.870 --> 00:31:20.030
So this one I talked about.

00:31:20.030 --> 00:31:22.777
Scalable-- I mentioned this.

00:31:22.777 --> 00:31:24.610
Tens of millions of
devices handled entirely

00:31:24.610 --> 00:31:25.235
from the cloud.

00:31:25.235 --> 00:31:27.402
You never need to worry
about adding new servers

00:31:27.402 --> 00:31:29.110
or anything to manage
your devices, which

00:31:29.110 --> 00:31:30.130
I think is great.

00:31:30.130 --> 00:31:36.060
There are several
deployments of Chromebooks

00:31:36.060 --> 00:31:40.080
with more than 100,000
devices in any organization,

00:31:40.080 --> 00:31:41.250
both schools and businesses.

00:31:41.250 --> 00:31:42.958
And what's actually
even more interesting

00:31:42.958 --> 00:31:46.800
than that is most of them didn't
hire a single additional IT

00:31:46.800 --> 00:31:52.410
person, which is staggering when
you think about 180,000 in one

00:31:52.410 --> 00:31:53.810
situation.

00:31:53.810 --> 00:31:55.880
And they didn't hire
anyone additional.

00:31:55.880 --> 00:31:56.550
Oh, good.

00:31:56.550 --> 00:31:57.360
Thank you so much.

00:31:57.360 --> 00:31:58.650
You can come see me.

00:31:58.650 --> 00:32:01.170
I'll probably be
here for one minute.

00:32:01.170 --> 00:32:03.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

